# Part 2 - Users and Devices Are Separate Objects

One of the most important concepts from this lab was separating **user identity** from **device identity**.

For example:

```text
jsmith = USER
COMP01 = DEVICE
```

These are separate directory objects.

A single managed computer may also have multiple users over its lifetime.

```text
                 COMP01
              Windows 11 PC
                   │
          ┌────────┼────────┐
          ▼        ▼        ▼
       jsmith    User2    Admin
```

Intune can apply settings based on either users or devices.

Examples of device-related management:

```text
COMP01 must have Windows Firewall enabled.
COMP01 must receive a configuration profile.
COMP01 must install a required application.
```

Examples of user-related management:

```text
jsmith belongs to SG-Sales.
jsmith receives applications assigned to SG-Sales.
jsmith receives user-targeted policies.
```

A user being associated with an Intune device does **not** mean the user and device are the same object.

---

# Part 3 - Intune Enrollment vs Microsoft Entra Join

Another major concept learned was that:

> Intune enrollment is not the same thing as Microsoft Entra Join.

A Windows device can be managed by Intune without being Microsoft Entra joined.

Before troubleshooting Hybrid Join, `COMP01` could already:

- Communicate with Intune
- Receive configuration profiles
- Receive compliance policies
- Perform Intune Sync
- Report device information
- Appear as a managed endpoint

However:

```powershell
dsregcmd /status
```

returned:

```text
AzureAdJoined    : NO
EnterpriseJoined : NO
DomainJoined     : YES
DomainName       : ADEN
Device Name      : comp01.aden.local
```

Therefore:

```text
Active Directory Domain Join    ✅
Intune Management               ✅
Microsoft Entra Device Join     ❌
```

This explained why Intune management could work even though the device itself did not yet have a Hybrid Microsoft Entra identity.

