# Part 7 - Configuring Hybrid Microsoft Entra Join

Microsoft Entra Connect Sync was opened on the server.

The following configuration path was used:

```text
Microsoft Entra Connect Sync
        ↓
Configure
        ↓
Configure device options
        ↓
Configure Hybrid Microsoft Entra ID join
```

For supported operating systems, the following option was selected:

```text
Windows 10 or later domain-joined devices
```

Windows 11 is included in this category.

---

# Part 8 - Configuring the Service Connection Point

The next step was configuring the **Service Connection Point (SCP)**.

The Active Directory forest selected was:

```text
aden.local
```

The authentication service was configured for Microsoft Entra ID.

On-premises Active Directory Enterprise Administrator credentials were used to authorize the SCP configuration.

This demonstrated another important distinction:

```text
Microsoft Entra Administrator
        │
        └── Manages cloud identity / Entra

Active Directory Enterprise Administrator
        │
        └── Manages the on-premises AD forest
```

Both administrative environments participate in Hybrid Microsoft Entra Join.

---

# What the SCP Does

The Service Connection Point allows domain-joined Windows computers to discover which Microsoft Entra tenant they should register with.

Conceptually:

```text
COMP01
   │
   │ "Which Entra tenant belongs to this domain?"
   ▼
Active Directory SCP
   │
   │ Tenant information
   ▼
Microsoft Entra ID
```

Microsoft Entra Connect successfully completed the configuration.

The wizard reported:

```text
Configuration complete

The task to configure Hybrid Microsoft Entra ID join
completed successfully.
```

---

# Part 9 - Device Registration Did Not Happen Immediately

After configuring Hybrid Microsoft Entra Join, the Windows 11 machine was checked again:

```powershell
dsregcmd /status
```

It still returned:

```text
AzureAdJoined : NO
DomainJoined  : YES
```

This demonstrated that configuring the infrastructure does not necessarily cause the endpoint to register instantly.

The next troubleshooting step was to investigate the Windows device registration process.

---

# Part 10 - Windows Automatic Device Join

Windows Task Scheduler was opened on `COMP01`.

The following path was inspected:

```text
Task Scheduler Library
    ↓
Microsoft
    ↓
Windows
    ↓
Workplace Join
```

Inside the folder was:

```text
Automatic-Device-Join
```

This scheduled task participates in automatic device registration.

The task was manually triggered to initiate another device registration attempt.

This was an important troubleshooting lesson:

> Configuring the server-side infrastructure and executing the endpoint-side registration process are separate steps.

---

# Part 11 - Successful Hybrid Microsoft Entra Join

After triggering `Automatic-Device-Join`, the device state was checked again:

```powershell
dsregcmd /status
```

The result changed to:

```text
AzureAdJoined    : YES
EnterpriseJoined : NO
DomainJoined     : YES
DomainName       : ADEN
Device Name      : comp01.aden.local
```

Additional information showed:

```text
DeviceAuthStatus : SUCCESS
TpmProtected     : YES
```

The important combination was:

```text
AzureAdJoined : YES
DomainJoined  : YES
```

This confirmed that `COMP01` was successfully:

# Hybrid Microsoft Entra Joined

